  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nUpper level troughing is in place with dry air aloft, with \nplentiful fair weather cumulus that has developed this \nafternoon. Any shower development through the day will struggle \nwith the dry air aloft mixing into the boundary layer so chances\nof precipitation were removed to not be in the grids. If a \nshower does pop up, they should not be impactful and mainly be \nlight sprinkles. Labor day is looking similar to today, even a \nlittle bit drier so there may not be as many clouds to form with\nday time heating. With high pressure in place leading to clear,\ncalm conditions overnight, typical valley fog is expected to \ndevelop each night through Tuesday morning. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nStill watching the potential for a stronger area of low \npressure to pass north of the Great Lakes Region in Southern \nCanada. The front has continued to trend weaker by the time it \nreaches the Northeast. It also has been slowing down as well \nwhich could at least mean better potential for widespread rain. \nSome of the deterministic models are trying to form a secondary \nlow along the front that propagates into the Northeast which \nwould really enhance the rainfall Wednesday night into Thursday.\nPWATs are up around 1.5 to 2 inches so there is an increasing \nthreat of heavy rain if that secondary low does form. Given the \nweakening of the front and timing is trending to be either \novernight Wednesday or early Thursday, the severe risk is \nlessening. Once the front or secondary low is through, we will \nbe back in a similar pattern as we are in now.",
